ollars on each of them  they should be able to go to cal state  fullerton if they chose but  because we have been so focused on that  i think you know like my son is in um  industrial arts program where they you  know build cars and solar boats and  he's been doing welding and metal shop  and all these things and he loves it and  in our district  it's we have 60 000 kids and there's  enough slots for maybe 300 kids to do  that you know like if kids were  interested in that there's just very few  opportunities and we've moved away from  expanding options in some ways and  that's the good thing about the school  choice movement is that there are niche  schools that have tried to go back to  choices that would lead to more work for  kids but you know we've focused away  from work  and toward seat based time for all kids  and i think that that has been because  of the work rules that have reduced  choice and innovation  okay um i'd like to take one for  actually standing in the very back there  which make jenna run around  so this is for anyone in the panel if my  supposition holds water it probably  affects all of you but lisa was from  your slide the first  graphic that you put up showed  1970 as a comparison point  and i think you could make an argument  that if 1970 education was good  those people that were educated in that  system  implemented everything that we're  finding flaws with now  could you would you make that point um  so you may be comparing something that's  bad against something that maybe was bad  so i mean 1970 largely is a data point  because  both it's when many things changed in  terms of the way  unions and collective bargaining happen  but also it's just an artifact right of  the data collection systems that we have  in that we became more focused on  testing and national data at that point  but you know i would argue that  the measurement at that time was not  good in terms of graduation rates and  student performance and when you look at  out