ers or nurses  and so the very best women in this  country the best brains  who happen to be women became teachers  and so we had this spectacular work  force back then  and  the women's revolution freed women to  become lawyers and doctors and  professors and all the rest and they did  right so now half the medical school  classes are women half the law school  classes are women this is a true  revolution but i think it's really had  an impact on the quality of the teaching  force  okay so that that's a major thing  uh another major thing is that the  demographics of the school population  have changed dramatically and so it's  really important in assessing  performance changes over time to  separate out these groups and take a  look at performance changes across  groups  it's a bad  a bad situation even if you look at just  white students  but it's not as bad as when you just  pool all these students together because  you would expect performance to to take  a hit just because the demographic  changes  well the red button has come on so we  have time for one more question and i'd  like to um let's say in the back there  right yes jenna yes yes you go yes  hi um no one's really touched on  parental involvement  i my daughters went to an elementary  school here in san diego with about 350  children  and i was on the governor's committee  which  took money from the state and decided  how to allocate it we had  less than 50 children in the school that  scored below  school  below the california guidelines  so we took that money those thousands of  dollars and put them into those kids and  over three years it didn't change at all  and my feeling was and as was some of  the other parents is that that money  that was funneled into those 50 kids  and taken away from the other 300 kids  went to children that didn't have  parents that were really involved there  was a language barrier in a lot of the  homes  and there just wasn't the um i don't  know appreciation or responsibility so  in everything that we've been talking  about you know we've i feel like  parents have kind of shifted the  responsibility onto the teachers and  under the schools  and when are the parents going to start  taking the responsibility back also  so  with parent i mean you're right  obviously like when the coleman report  in 1966 i mean parents and home  environment is a difficult challenge and  always will be  so then it  comes what are the best kinds of  institutional structures  that  will work  to mitigate things that happen to kids  at home so clearly  the more centralized monopolistic union  controlled structures  don't  they alienate parents even more so the  kind of school that you're describing  even though your investment in the end  didn't pay off the fact even that you  could  analyze as a parent how those resources  were being spent and how those decisions  were being made is still an improvement  over because you at least knew  right that your investment wasn't paying  off and then you can try to make other  decisions moving forward which in most  schools in america there's nobody  looking at did we make this extra  investment in these 50 kids and did what  we were doing help no one's asking that  question at all so i think the  autonomous schools that terry talks  about without  where they have a performance framework  where there's expectations  where parent where the people truly  closest to the kids the parents parent  committees the principals the teachers  that they're not always going to have  the right incentives but they have  better incentives than institutions that  are really far removed from making  decisions about what happens in the  school so in our large study i mean it  really showed that  the districts that removed work rules  that had collective bargaining relief  that gave principals a lot of the money  that use school site councils that in  general though on a variety of  indicators those districts are making  progress in c